{
  "dom_id": "step-q212931",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA=\\{Z,Q,R\\}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nX\\,R\\,Y\\,⇔\\,X⊇Y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $R\\,R\\,Z$\n\nII. $Q\\,R\\,R$\n\nIII. $Q\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,Z$",
  "worked_solution": "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $X\\,R\\,Y$ if and only if $X⊇Y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $⊇$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $⊉$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $⊇,$ we have $R⊇Z.$ This is equivalent to $Z⊆R,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $⊇,$ we have $Q⊇R.$ This is equivalent to $R⊆Q,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement III is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $⊉,$ we have $Q⊉Z.$ This is equivalent to $Z⊈Q,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
